  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 PM EDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nSynoptic pattern through Friday evening features broad upper \ntroughing centered over the Upper Midwest and western Great Lakes, \nwith moist conditions embedded in weak southwest flow. This \nmoist and somewhat unstable environment will continue to support\nperiodic chances for scattered showers and isolated storms with\nany small scale perturbations and outflows embedded in this \nweak flow, best chances (30-60%) during the afternoon and early \nevening hours today and Friday. Lightning and locally heavy \nrainfall the main hazards with any convection given the lacking \nflow/shear through the column. Patchy fog remains possible again\notherwise later tonight, mainly north of the US 24 corridor in \nthe vicinity of a weak sfc front. \n\nThe main frontal boundary draped from the Plains northeast into the \nlower Great Great Lakes will get a push southeast through the area \non Saturday as the aforementioned upstream broad trough finally \nreleases eastward. Latest timing favors locations along and east of \nthe Interstate 69 corridor by Saturday afternoon for at least a\nlow chance (20-30%) for convection along the boundary. Drier \nair briefly filters in post-frontal elsewhere on Saturday, and \nacross the entire forecast area into Saturday night with mainly \ndry conditions anticipated. \n\nThe pattern aloft Sunday into much of next week will transition to \nlow amplitude west-northwest flow on the northern periphery of a \nsouthern CONUS subtropical ridge, while in the low levels a frontal \nzone and associated theta-e gradient will be draped from the Upper \nMidwest southeast into the lower Great Lakes and Ohio Valley. This \nregime likely keeps the more oppressive heat off to the south with \ntemperatures close to where they should be for this time of year. \nDaily shower/storm chances were also retained during these periods \nwith the frontal zone in the vicinity.",
